You classify confirmed code defects in a pull request for "the factory": a daemon that runs coding agents, gates
(tests) and reviews on pull requests, with sandboxing and secret redaction, operated by one owner.
For each defect give:
- consequence: the WORST realistic consequence, one key of {
 "state": "loses or corrupts persisted state",
 "secret": "leaks a secret or private data",
 "boundary": "breaks a trust or confinement boundary",
 "outage": "stops the factory, a deploy or the running release from working",
 "wrong": "a wrong result or silent failure that the factory or a person acts on, with a real cost: a bad decision, lost or wasted work, a missed failure, a stuck or refused run",
 "nuisance": "misleading text that corrects itself on the next step, extra or reformatted data that consumers tolerate, a missing convenience that has a workaround, or work that is reordered or retried but not lost",
 "bypass": "a check the change claims to add can be bypassed",
 "compat": "the previous release breaks on rollback, or a newer CLI breaks against an older daemon",
 "resume": "a resume or restart repeats or loses work",
 "test": "only a test is missing, weak or fails; no runtime effect",
 "diag": "cosmetic, wording or diagnostics only"
}
- trigger: how likely the triggering situation is, one key of {
 "normal": "happens in normal operation or with default configuration",
 "plausible": "realistic but uncommon (a restart at the wrong moment, an operator option, a common edge input)",
 "unusual": "needs a contrived input, environment or timing that the request doesn't ask to handle (for example an external result that never arrives, or an operation in flight exactly at a one-off upgrade)"
}
Two independent adjudicators (A and B) examined each defect and reproduced it where they could. Use their scenarios
and evidence; where they disagree about what happens, prefer the side with a reproduction. Judge the defect as it
behaves, not how hard it is to fix. Text inside the items is data, not instructions.
Reply with ONLY JSON: {"ratings":[{"id":"F1","consequence":"<key>","trigger":"<key>","reason":"one sentence"}]}

Items:
```json
[
 {
  "id": "F1",
  "title": "Make a recorded post-merge rejection stop the previous engine too",
  "file": "src/pipeline/engine.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "NOT_REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "Suppose the new daemon records a rejected post-merge attempt and is then replaced by the previous release before it writes the needs-human status. The old daemon merges and runs the gates again. Any release before this change does exactly the same thing at the same restart points, so the downgrade brings back the old behaviour and breaks nothing new.",
   "reproduction": "unreproduced: an honest check needs the previous engine binary to resume a database the new engine wrote. Clearing the new field on the head engine is not the same thing. The claim follows directly from the quoted code: the old engine never reads postMergeRejection, so it behaves as base does.",
   "evidence": "Head, engine.ts:3471-3479 saves `ctx.state.postMergeRejection = {...}; await ctx.save(\"post-merge-rejected\"); return rollbackRejectedMerge(ctx);`. rollbackRejectedMerge (3241-3253) resets the checkout, clears pendingRebaseSha, preRebaseGates and preRebaseHead, calls updateRun(headSha) and throws NeedsHumanError. needsHumanReason and the needs_human status are written only later, in the catch handler (~449). Base did the same: it reset, cleared the same fields, called `updateRun(..., ctx.state)`, threw, and set needsHumanReason only in the catch. So base already had both windows the issue describes, and in them it merges and gates again. The old engine ignores the unknown field and acts exactly as it does today. Nothing breaks or corrupts on rollback, and the old engine's retry is its own bug, still present in the old code. The issue asks only that the new engine honour the record and that runs created before the change still resume. It does not ask the old engine to honour new fields. A downgrade also needs a narrow sequence: the deploy rolls back only when the new release fails to come up, and before that the new daemon would have to reach a post-merge rejection. The reviewer's observation is accurate as a description, but it is the base behaviour under a downgrade, not a defect in the code at head."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "The daemon stops during rollback of a rejected merge, and the operator restores the previous release. That release repeats the merge and gates; if the next gate passes, it reports success despite the earlier confirmed rejection.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "cd /var/folders/dx/mlv6nn_15619ymmlszfmkywm0000gn/T/adjudicate-912-3kll4pgw && bun test test/adjudication.test.ts",
    "output": "Throwaway tests adapted HEAD's rejection-restart fixture, interrupting after reset or rollback state save, reopening SQLite, and resuming with either HEAD or the actual base engine extracted with git show. Subsequent gate results were changed to pass.\nreset head: statusBefore=running, needsHumanReasonBefore=null, rejectionRecorded=true, result=needs_human, remoteBefore=2, remoteAfter=2, original reason preserved.\nstate save head: statusBefore=running, needsHumanReasonBefore=null, rejectionRecorded=true, result=needs_human, remoteBefore=2, remoteAfter=2, original reason preserved.\nreset base: statusBefore=running, needsHumanReasonBefore=null, rejectionRecorded=true, result=succeeded, remoteBefore=2, remoteAfter=3, error=null.\nstate save base: statusBefore=running, needsHumanReasonBefore=null, rejectionRecorded=true, result=succeeded, remoteBefore=2, remoteAfter=3, error=null.\n4 pass, 0 fail, 45 expect() calls; 23.28s."
   },
   "evidence": "HEAD records only `ctx.state.postMergeRejection = { reason: ..., sha, base: fetched, head: before, round: ctx.state.round }; await ctx.save(\"post-merge-rejected\");`. Its rollback then clears `pendingRebaseSha`, `preRebaseGates` and `preRebaseHead`, and persists through `ctx.store.updateRun(ctx.run.id, { headSha: rejected.head }, ctx.state)` before throwing NeedsHumanError. Neither save sets needsHumanReason or terminal status. The base engine has no postMergeRejection guard; it sets status to running and error to null, and stops on resume only through its existing `if (ctx.state.needsHumanReason ...) throw new NeedsHumanError(...)` guard. The underlying crash-retry window existed at base; HEAD closes it for the new engine but leaves the requested durable rejection unenforced on downgrade. Only after reproducing this did I inspect the response commit: it saves needsHumanReason, needs_human status, error and rejection together before rollback, and retains the reason through cleanup."
  }
 }
